---
import HagueDoc from '../../../layouts/HagueDoc.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is a same-name declaration?',
    a: 'A one-and-the-same-person statement: it proves that the English name on your passport, the name on your Chinese ID, and the name on a property deed or hukou are the same person. Housing bureaus, notary offices and banks in China often stop a file here. Call 647-930-6688.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I skip this if I already have a Power of Attorney?',
    a: 'Only if every name matches exactly. Pinyin, a former name, traditional characters, or a missing middle name often trigger a separate same-person declaration. Miao He reviews the IDs before deciding whether both documents should go out together.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is next-day pickup available for this file too?',
    a: 'Yes, urgent originals can be collected the next day. Recent same-name declarations filed with property POAs were accepted on the first pass. Call 647-930-6688 to confirm the slot.',
  },
];
---
<HagueDoc lang="en" slug="same-name-declaration" faqs={faqs}>
  <div class="definition-box">
    <p>Alongside recent China-property Powers of Attorney, Miao He apostilled <strong>same-name / same-person declarations</strong> for clients whose passport, Chinese ID or property-deed names do not match. That mismatch is where filings in China often stop. The drafts were accepted on the first pass. Urgent originals were available the next day.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>When you need it</h2>
  <p>The passport is in pinyin. The Chinese ID is in characters. The property deed may use a former name, traditional characters, or drop a middle name. A housing bureau, notary office or bank in China will ask for an apostilled declaration that these records belong to one person.</p>
  <ul class="content-list">
    <li>selling, buying or registering title when the passport name and the registered name differ;</li>
    <li>inheritance or a waiver of succession when hukou, ID and Canadian documents do not match;</li>
    <li>a bank or securities firm that requires an explanation of Chinese and English names.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Miao He is a dual-licensed litigation lawyer. She lists each name as it appears on each document, instead of a one-line “I am the same person.”</p>

  <h2>What to bring</h2>
  <ul class="content-list">
    <li>passport, Chinese ID, PR card or citizenship card;</li>
    <li>the property deed, hukou, old passport or former-name proof;</li>
    <li>copies of the China documents that have to be matched.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>This declaration is often issued together with a <a href="/en/hague-apostille/china-property-power-of-attorney/">China property Power of Attorney</a>. Drafting, notarizing and apostilling them as one file is cleaner than running two separate trips.</p>
</HagueDoc>
